# Monitor Brightness Switch

One-click dim and restore for every connected monitor, sent over DDC/CI. It comes as a tray icon, optional taskbar buttons and one small PowerShell script. You don't touch the monitors' on-screen menus, and you don't install any extra software.

## Features
- **Dim / restore in one click.** Every monitor drops to its own lowest brightness, and the next click puts each one back at the exact level it had before.
- **Tray icon next to the clock.** A thin `-` while the screens are lit (click = dim) and a `+` while they are dimmed (click = restore). Right-click for a menu with 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 %, and hover to see each monitor's level.
- **Monitors stay in sync.** Some monitors jump to a new brightness instantly while others fade on their own. The script moves all of them through the same values at the same time, so they look the same.
- **Checks its own work.** After a change it reads each monitor back and re-sends the value if a monitor silently dropped it.
- **Any number of monitors.** Plain Windows APIs, so no drivers, no admin rights and no third-party tools.

## Tech stack
Windows PowerShell 5.1 (built into Windows 10/11), C# compiled at runtime with `Add-Type` (P/Invoke into `dxva2.dll`), Windows Forms `NotifyIcon` for the tray, and VBScript launchers so nothing flashes a console window.

## Requirements
- Windows 10 or 11 with external monitors that have **DDC/CI** switched on. It's on by default on most monitors, but check the monitor's own menu if `status` shows nothing.
- Laptop built-in panels don't speak DDC/CI, so they aren't covered.
- A few docks, KVMs and cheap adapters block DDC/CI. A direct DisplayPort or HDMI cable is the safe choice.

Tested on Windows 11 with two external monitors.

## Getting started
```powershell
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo>.git
cd <repo>

# 1. check that your monitors answer
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\dim.ps1 status

# 2. start the tray icon (hidden, no console window)
wscript .\brightness-tray.vbs
```

**Start the tray at sign-in** (a shortcut in your Startup folder; delete it to undo):
```powershell
$here = (Get-Location).Path
$s = (New-Object -ComObject WScript.Shell).CreateShortcut("$([Environment]::GetFolderPath('Startup'))\Brightness Tray.lnk")
$s.TargetPath = "$env:WINDIR\System32\wscript.exe"
$s.Arguments = "`"$here\brightness-tray.vbs`""
$s.WorkingDirectory = $here
$s.Save()
```

**Optional pinned taskbar buttons.** Create one shortcut per action, then right-click it, choose *Show more options*, then *Pin to taskbar*. Windows doesn't let scripts pin for you.
```powershell
$here = (Get-Location).Path
$s = (New-Object -ComObject WScript.Shell).CreateShortcut("$here\Brightness -.lnk")
$s.TargetPath = "$env:WINDIR\System32\wscript.exe"
$s.Arguments = "`"$here\brightness.vbs`" down"            # use "up" for the restore button
$s.IconLocation = "$here\Taskbar\brightness-down.ico,0"   # brightness-up.ico for restore
$s.Save()
```

## Usage
```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\dim.ps1 <action> [-Seconds <n>]
```

| Action | What it does |
|---|---|
| `toggle` (default) | Lowest brightness, or back to the remembered level if everything is already dimmed |
| `down` | Lowest brightness and remember the previous level |
| `up` | Restore the remembered level (50 % if nothing is remembered) |
| `0`-`100` | Set every monitor to that percentage |
| `status` | Show each monitor's current level |

`-Seconds` sets how long the fade takes (default 1, `0` = instant).

```text
> .\dim.ps1 down
Dimming to lowest:
  [0] Generic PnP Monitor 100 -> 0
  [1] Generic PnP Monitor 100 -> 0

> .\dim.ps1 status
  [0] Generic PnP Monitor 0/100
  [1] Generic PnP Monitor 0/100
```

**Tray menu:** Dim to lowest, Restore previous level, 25 %, 50 %, 75 %, 100 %, Exit. The menu follows Windows light/dark mode.
**Double-click buttons:** `brightness -.cmd` and `brightness +.cmd` do the same as `down` and `up`. They look for `dim.ps1` in their own folder, so keep them together.

## How it works
- Brightness is VCP feature `0x10` in the DDC/CI standard. `dim.ps1` finds every physical monitor with `EnumDisplayMonitors` and talks to it with `GetVCPFeatureAndVCPFeatureReply` and `SetVCPFeature`.
- Fades are time based: all monitors are written back to back as fast as the bus allows, then the final value is written, read back and re-sent if it didn't stick.
- The level before dimming is kept in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\dim\state.json`. It's only overwritten with levels above 0, so dimming twice never loses the real value.
- The tray app is a thin shell. It draws its own `+` / `-` icon at the exact tray size for the current theme, and runs `dim.ps1` as a child process, one change at a time (a click during a change waits its turn). It re-reads the state when the menu opens and after every change.

## Project structure
```text
dim.ps1                     the engine: reads, sets and fades brightness
brightness-tray.ps1         tray icon + menu
brightness-tray.vbs         starts the tray hidden (one copy at a time)
brightness.vbs              hidden runner for taskbar buttons: wscript brightness.vbs down|up|toggle
brightness -.cmd / +.cmd    double-click versions of down / up
Make-Icons.ps1              redraws the + / - icons for the current Windows theme
Taskbar/                    icons for the pinned buttons
```

## Security notes
- No network access, no admin rights. Everything runs as the current user.
- The only commands sent to a monitor are read and write of brightness (`0x10`).
- The launchers pass `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` to the single PowerShell process they start. It doesn't change any machine or user policy. If that doesn't suit you, sign the scripts and run them under your own policy.
- Files written: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\dim\state.json` (remembered level) and `tray-error.log` next to it. Nothing else is changed. The Startup shortcut exists only if you create it.
- The tray runs `dim.ps1` from its own folder at every click, so treat the folder like any autostart target and keep it writable only by you.

## Troubleshooting
- **`status` shows an error or a "did not answer" warning:** turn DDC/CI on in the monitor's own menu, then try a direct cable instead of a dock or adapter.
- **A change reports `FAILED` or "would not confirm":** DDC/CI is flaky on some hardware. The script already retries; running it again usually works.
- **Monitors show up as "Generic PnP Monitor":** that's the name Windows reports for them. The order matches Windows' monitor enumeration.
- **Pinned icon looks wrong after switching light/dark mode:** run `.\Make-Icons.ps1`, then restart Explorer if it still looks stale.

## Roadmap
- [ ] Installer script that creates the Startup entry and taskbar shortcuts
- [ ] Per-monitor control in the tray menu
- [ ] Scheduled dim / restore (for example, at night)
- [ ] Show the real monitor names from EDID instead of "Generic PnP Monitor"
- [ ] Laptop built-in panels through WMI

## What was tricky
- **Monitors don't react alike.** One jumped straight to the new level while the other faded by itself. That's firmware, so the fix was to ramp every monitor through the same values together.
- **DDC/CI is unreliable.** Reads sometimes fail and a write can be dropped without any error. The script retries reads, then reads every monitor back and re-sends if it didn't stick.
- **Positions matter.** Values are matched to monitors by position, so one monitor failing a read must never shift the list. A bug where a command went to the wrong monitor showed up in testing and was fixed by keeping every monitor in the list.
- **.NET can't read PNG-compressed `.ico` frames,** so the tray draws its own icon at runtime instead of loading a file.

FOSS, enjoy.
